[THOUGHT] The patient lacks recall or access to key exposure and substance use history — common in elderly or cognitively impaired individuals (though not yet assessed). Since domain 6 is incomplete but further probing may yield little, I’ll move to domain 7: targeted physical examination. This is high-yield and discriminating: focal leg weakness must be localized neuroanatomically. I need to assess strength (graded 0–5), tone, reflexes (patellar, Achilles), plantar response (Babinski), gait (observe for foot drop, steppage, ataxia, hemiparesis), and sensory exam (light touch, pinprick, vibration — though patient denied symptoms, objective testing may reveal deficits). Also, look for signs of HIV-related complications: oral thrush, lymphadenopathy, skin lesions (KS), or retinal findings (CMV retinitis — though not directly related to leg weakness, it signals severe immunosuppression).

[TEST] Perform a targeted neurological examination including:  
- Manual muscle testing of right lower extremity (hip flexion/extension, knee extension/flexion, ankle dorsiflexion/plantarflexion, toe extension/flexion) bilaterally, graded 0–5;  
- Deep tendon reflexes (patellar and Achilles) bilaterally;  
- Babinski sign;  
- Gait observation (tandem, heel-to-toe, ability to rise from chair without arms);  
- Light touch and pinprick sensation in L2–S1 dermatomes bilaterally.

